  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 144 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows the upper level low diving southeast \nacross Nevada. The low will bottom out over northern Arizona \ntonight and be centered over the Four Corners 18Z Wednesday. Water\nvapor imagery and radar show an area of lift over eastern Utah \nand western Colorado, with widespread showers. This area of lift \nshifts eastward tonight bringing rain and mountain snow showers to\nthe western two-thirds of Colorado. Easterly low level flow will \ncontinue tonight and Wednesday around the low to our southwest and\na surface high to the north. Cross sections show that the low \nlevels will become saturated overnight and Wednesday. This \nsaturated layer becomes deep enough for drizzle late tonight and \nWednesday. The upper level low tracks northeast across Colorado \nWednesday afternoon through Wednesday night bringing widespread \nrain and high country snow. The best snowfall for the mountains \ncomes later Wednesday and Wednesday night when flow becomes a \nfavorable northwest direction (for orographic lift). We've issued \na Winter Weather Advisory for the mountains where 6 to 12 inches \nof snow are expected.\u00a0The snow level tonight will be 10,000 to \n11,000 feet dipping below 10,000 feet over the northern mountains \ntowards sunrise Wednesday morning. Once the low moves over \nColorado, the snow level falls to 8,000 feet around sunset \nWednesday evening and 7,500 feet by Thursday morning.\u00a0 Rain and \nsnow linger into Thursday, especially across northern Colorado, \nwrapping up during the afternoon.\u00a0\u00a0\n\nSkies clear out around sunset Thursday night as a surface settles\nover Colorado. This will bring the coldest night so far this \nfall. Many places will see a frost or freeze Thursday night and \nFriday morning. Overnight lows are on track to be in the upper 20s\nto mid 30s over northeast Colorado. Typical low lying areas/cold \nspots are expected to fall into the mid to lower 20s.\n\nAn upper level high will be centered over the intermountain west,\nwith a closed low trapped beneath\u00a0it off the coast of northwest \nMexico on Friday. Cross sections show a very dry airmass, \nresulting in sunny skies all day on Friday. Cool air lingers with \nhighs in the upper 50s to lower 60s, but light winds and sunny \nskies will make it feel warmer. The upper level ridge slides east \nof the region Saturday. Southwest\u00a0flow aloft will prevail behind \nthe ridge bringing warmer air to the area. Temperatures climb \nabove normal with highs expected to reach upper 60s to lower 70s \nacross northeast Colorado.\u00a0\n\nA weak trough in the flow aloft tracks across the Central Rockies\non Sunday. It also absorbs the upper level low off the northwest \nMexico coast.\u00a0 Models indicate very little moisture will accompany\nthis trough, thus dry conditions are expected to continue Sunday.\nTemperatures may take a small step back.\u00a0\n\nMild and dry conditions are expected for Monday and\u00a0Tuesday as\nsouthwest flow redevelops over the Central Rockies ahead of a\ndeepening trough along the west coast. This trough tracks eastward\nmid to late next week bringing the next chance for rain, mountain\nsnow, and cooler temperatures.",
